  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "A planned failover of the edge firewall pair to the secondary unit created an asymmetric network path: outbound traffic went through the secondary firewall while return traffic still routed through the primary. Each stateful firewall only saw one direction of a connection, so it dropped the 'unexpected' return packets. Connections needing many round-trips (large uploads, streaming responses) failed with mid-transfer TCP resets at ~40%, while short request/response pairs usually completed before hitting the problem. The trigger is the failover leaving routing asymmetric. Recovery required correcting the return route to match the outbound path.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "network-ops INFO at 09:15: planned failover of the edge firewall pair to the secondary completed",
      "region-gateway WARN: elevated connection resets on long-lived flows while short requests are mostly fine",
      "large uploads and streaming responses fail ~40% with mid-transfer TCP resets",
      "packet capture shows return traffic via the primary firewall and outbound via the secondary - an asymmetric path; stateful inspection drops the return packets",
      "recovery after the return route is corrected to match outbound"
    ],
    "fix": "Correct the return route so both directions traverse the same firewall (done). Make firewall failover switch both directions atomically, add a post-failover synthetic check for large-transfer and long-lived connections, and enable stateful-session sync between the firewall pair.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
